ns intersect the hole block (i,i). Since holes are inside that block, the rectangle would contain holes. So we must avoid including column group i for any row group i in the row interval. Thus row interval and column interval must be such that they are disjoint as sets of indices. Since row interval includes many indices, column interval must avoid those indices. So column interval must be a contiguous interval of column groups that is entirely within the complement of row interval indices. If row interval is a contiguous segment [a,b], then column interval must be a contiguous segment that lies entirely in [1,a-1] or [b+1,k]. So we can have rectangle covering rows groups [a,b] and columns groups [b+1,k] or [1,a-1]. That yields a rectangle covering top-right or bottom-left off-diagonal region relative to the selected row interval.\n\nThus we can partition the off-diagonal region into at most 2(k-1) rectangles: For each possible row interval we could cover a block. But we can do more clever partitioning.\n\nActually, we can tile the off-diagonal region using (2k - 2) rectangles at group level, analogous to the original triangular tiling but now each rectangle is a super-rectangle covering multiple groups. For each group index i, we can have rectangle covering rows groups i..k and columns groups i+1..k (above diagonal) -> that's k-1 rectangles. Similarly, rectangles covering rows groups i+1..k and columns groups i..i (?), not exactly. Let's think.\n\nBut we could also tile each off-diagonal block individually (k(k-1) blocks) using each block as a rectangle (size k x k). That's k(k-1) rectangles, which is O(k^2) = n, larger than 2n-2 = O(n). So not beneficial.\n\nThus the grouping approach does not reduce complexity at group level; we still have O(k^2) rectangles needed to cover off-diagonal region, unless we can combine groups into larger rectangles crossing many groups at once. But the constraint that row interval and column interval must be disjoint sets of indices means that we can cover all off-diagonal cells with exactly 2(k-1) rectangles as before: for each i from 1 to k-1, rectangle covering rows groups i..k and columns groups i+1..k (above diagonal). Those are k-1 rectangles. Similarly, rectangles covering rows groups i+1..k and columns groups i..i? Actually that's same as above after symmetry, but we can also cover below diagonal with k-1 rectangles. So total 2(k-1). However each of those super-rectangles corresponds to a rectangle covering many groups, thus covering many cells (including many blocks). Indeed, rectangle covering rows groups i..k and columns groups i+1..k includes all off-diagonal blocks in the lower-right submatrix of groups (i..k) x (i+1..k). That includes many blocks. So we can cover all off-diagonal cells with 2(k-1) rectangles, not k(k-1). So that reduces number from O(k^2) to O(k). Good.\n\nNow what about diagonal blocks (i,i) that contain holes? Within each diagonal block, we have to tile the block's interior (size k x k with one hole per row and column). That is exactly the same problem but of size k = sqrt(n). So we can recursively apply the same strategy. That suggests a recursive tiling scheme: treat the matrix as k blocks along diagonal, each block of size k x k with a permutation matrix of holes (maybe identity). Then tile each block recursively using the same approach. This yields a recursion for the number of tiles T(n) = 2(k-1) + k * T(k), where k = sqrt(n). For n = k^2. Since the off-diagonal region can be tiled with 2(k-1) rectangles (super-rectangles) that avoid holes because they are off-diagonal blocks. Then each diagonal block requires T(k) tiles.\n\nThus we have recurrence T(k^2) = 2(k-1) + k * T(k). Base case T(1) = 0 (since no cells to tile). This is reminiscent of a recursion for minimal number of rectangles.\n\nNow we can attempt to solve this recurrence for n = 45^2 = 2025.\n\nLet's compute T(45). Actually we need to compute T(k) for k=45, where k itself may be further factora