ment (holes) into rectangles. Equivalent: we have a grid with a forbidden cell in each row and column; we must tile the rest with rectangles.\n\nWe can think in terms of the graph representation: each rectangle corresponds to a submatrix (set of contiguous rows and columns) that avoids holes. The holes are points that break the grid. The rectangles must be axis-aligned contiguous blocks of cells that contain no holes.\n\nGoal: partition the grid minus the holes into the smallest number of axis-aligned rectangles (submatrices) whose interiors avoid holes.\n\nThis is similar to problem of covering a matrix by submatrices that avoid a set of forbidden entries. The holes are like obstacles. Because each tile cannot cross a hole; the presence of holes splits the grid.\n\nObservations:\n\n- If there were no holes, one tile would cover the whole grid (1 tile). With holes, each hole may cause many tiles. But we can possibly arrange holes in a pattern that allows large rectangles.\n\n- The holes are each in distinct rows and columns. They form a permutation matrix. So they lie on the main diagonal after reordering rows/columns.\n\n- If we reorder rows and columns (i.e., permute row indices and column indices), the set of holes can be moved to become the main diagonal (i,i). The tiling problem is invariant under reordering? We can reorder rows/columns globally, but the tile axis orientation must still be aligned with grid lines. If we permute rows/columns globally, we are essentially applying a permutation reordering of the rows and columns of the entire grid, which changes the geometry of the problem. However, we can think of the problem as: we can choose a permutation for holes; we can also permute rows and columns of the entire grid before tiling (i.e., we can relabel rows and columns). Since we are free to choose the permutation \u03c0, we can assume it's the identity (holes on the main diagonal) because any permutation can be transformed to identity by reordering rows an