ludes cells (i,j) with i < j; region below includes (i,j) with i > j. These are like two triangles, each is a Young diagram shape. The region above diagonal is upper triangular (strictly). That region can be tiled by rectangles: we could tile each row i's cells to the right of diagonal (columns i+1..n). That would be n - i cells, contiguous horizontally. So each row i (i from 1..n) we could have a horizontal rectangle covering (i, i+1..n). That's n rows -> n rectangles (except maybe last row where none). Similarly for region below diagonal, we could tile each column j's cells below diagonal (rows j+1..n). That's also n rectangles. But note that this double counts the region? Actually the upper region is covered by horizontal rectangles per row; the lower region is covered by vertical rectangles per column. So total rectangles = (n-1) + (n-1) = 2n - 2? Wait, row i for i=1..n, there are n-i cells; for i=n, zero cells, so we can ignore row n. So we have n-1 horizontal rectangles. Similarly, for lower region: column j has cells with rows j+1..n, which is n-j cells; for j=n, zero, so n-1 vertical rectangles. Total rectangles = 2(n-1) = 2n - 2.\n\nBut can we do better? Possibly we can combine some of the horizontal and vertical rectangles into larger rectangles that cross the diagonal but avoid holes? However any rectangle that contains both cells above and below diagonal must cross the diagonal line (i=j) somewhere. To avoid holes, it must avoid the diagonal cells, which are holes. So any rectangle cannot contain any hole; thus it cannot include any cell (i,i). But it could be shaped like a rectangle that lies entirely in the region above diagonal (i<j), or entirely below diagonal (i>j), or maybe straddle the diagonal but with a missing column/row? Let's examine.\n\nIf we try to tile the whole region above diagonal with fewer than n-1 rectangles, how can we? The region above diagonal is a shape known as a Ferrers diagram: for each row i, there are n-i cells in that row. 