interval covering multiple groups, e.g., rows from group g1 to g2 inclusive (some contiguous set of groups). Similarly column interval covering some set of groups. The condition is that for any row in the row interval, its hole column must be outside the column interval. If the row interval contains groups that intersect the column interval groups, then some rows may have holes within column interval (if the column interval includes the same group as the row's group). So the rectangle cannot include any row whose group's column block overlaps the column interval. So the rectangle's row group set and column group set must be disjoint (as groups). So essentially we can treat groups as super-rows and super-columns. The condition reduces to: The rectangle's row groups must be a set G_R, column groups a set G_C, with G_R \u2229 G_C = \u2205. Then rectangle can include all rows of those groups and all columns of those groups. So we can tile the grid at group level using rectangles (super-rectangles) that avoid intersecting diagonal groups.\n\nThus at group level, we have a k x k grid where each cell is a block of size k x k. The diagonal cells (g,g) contain holes (some holes within). Off-diagonal blocks are hole-free (since holes only in diagonal blocks). So the off-diagonal blocks can be covered by single large rectangles that combine multiple blocks as long as the rectangle's row group interval and column group interval avoid diagonal blocks. Actually if we take rectangle covering rows groups from a to b and column groups from c to d, with intervals (a..b) and (c..d). If intervals overlap (i.e., there is some group index in both intervals), then the rectangle will include diagonal blocks where holes exist, which is not allowed. So we need intervals that are disjoint. So we can have rectangle covering rows groups [1..p] and columns groups [p+1..k] for some p. That includes all blocks above the block diagonal (in group-level). That rectangle covers all off-diagonal blocks