          "reasoning_content": "We need to understand the problem precisely: We have a 2025 x 2025 grid of unit squares, with coordinates (i,j) for rows 1..2025, columns 1..2025. We need to place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned, sides on grid lines) covering some unit squares, such that each unit square is covered at most once (no overlap). Each row has exactly one uncovered unit square (i.e., each row has exactly one hole). Similarly each column has exactly one uncovered unit square.\n\nThus the set of uncovered unit squares forms a set of 2025 squares such that each row and column contains exactly one uncovered square. That means the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: one per row, one per column, i.e., a set of positions (i, \u03c0(i)) for some permutation \u03c0 of {1,...,2025}.\n\nGoal: tile the rest of the grid (all other unit squares) with axis-aligned rectangles (tiles), covering all other squares exactly once (since at most one tile per square, but we aim to cover everything except the holes). We can use rectangles of any size (including 1x1? Probably allowed). We need to minimize the number of tiles used.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: given a 2025 x 2025 grid with a set of 2025 hole squares forming a permutation matrix, we need to partition the remaining cells into axis-aligned rectangles (tiles). Find the minimum number of rectangles needed over all possible placements of holes (i.e., choice of permutation) and over all tilings. Since each row has exactly one hole, each column exactly one hole, the holes are a permutation. The minimal number of rectangles may depend on the permutation. We can choose the permutation to minimize the number of rectangles needed. The problem wants the minimum possible number of tiles across all strategies (choice of holes placement and tiling). We need to compute that min.\n\nWe need to find a lower bound and a construction achieving it.\n\nThis is reminiscent of the problem of partitioning a complete grid with a rook placement (holes) into rectangles. Equivalent: we have a grid with a forbidden cell in each row and column; we must tile the rest with rectangles.\n\nWe can think in terms of the graph representation: each rectangle corresponds to a submatrix (set of contiguous rows and columns) that avoids holes. The holes are points that break the grid. The rectangles must be axis-aligned contiguous blocks of cells that contain no holes.\n\nGoal: partition the grid minus the holes into the smallest number of axis-aligned rectangles (submatrices) whose interiors avoid holes.\n\nThis is similar to problem of covering a matrix by submatrices that avoid a set of forbidden entries. The holes are like obstacles. Because each tile cannot cross a hole; the presence of holes splits the grid.\n\nObservations:\n\n- If there were no holes, one tile would cover the whole grid (1 tile). With holes, each hole may cause many tiles. But we can possibly arrange holes in a pattern that allows large rectangles.\n\n- The holes are each in distinct rows and columns. They form a permutation matrix. So they lie on the main diagonal after reordering rows/columns.\n\n- If we reorder rows and columns (i.e., permute row indices and column indices), the set of holes can be moved to become the main diagonal (i,i). The tiling problem is invariant under reordering? We can reorder rows/columns globally, but the tile axis orientation must still be aligned with grid lines. If we permute rows/columns globally, we are essentially applying a permutation reordering of the rows and columns of the entire grid, which changes the geometry of the problem. However, we can think of the problem as: we can choose a permutation for holes; we can also permute rows and columns of the entire grid before tiling (i.e., we can relabel rows and columns). Since we are free to choose the permutation \u03c0, we can assume it's the identity (holes on the main diagonal) because any permutation can be transformed to identity by reordering rows and columns accordingly. But we need to verify that the tiling count is invariant under simultaneous permutations of rows and columns? Actually, if we apply a permutation to rows (reorder them) and a possibly different permutation to columns, we are rearranging the grid, but the geometry changes: the relative positions of holes change, but the adjacency structure of cells (which cells are adjacent horizontally/vertically) changes because we are permuting rows (i.e., swapping entire rows). However, swapping rows does not change adjacency horizontally (within a row) but changes vertical adjacency across rows: the order of rows matters for contiguity of vertical blocks. So the tiling problem is not invariant under arbitrary row permutations. However, we are free to choose the permutation of holes; but we cannot arbitrarily reorder rows after placing holes because that changes the geometry. But we can decide to place holes in any permutation pattern. So we can choose the best pattern for minimal tiling. Usually the best pattern is to place holes all in one diagonal (like identity). But not sure if there is a better arrangement.\n\nWe need to find the minimum number of rectangles needed to tile an n x n grid with a permutation matrix of holes.\n\nLet n = 2025 = 45^2.\n\nGoal: find min number T(n) of rectangles needed (tiles) to partition the grid minus n holes with one per row and column.\n\nWe suspect answer is something like 2n - 1? Or something like n + something? Let's explore.\n\nIf we place holes along the main diagonal, we can tile the grid in a simple way: we can take each row i, the cells left of the hole (columns 1..i-1) can be tiled as a rectangle covering rows i..n? Not exactly. Let's think.\n\nIf holes are on main diagonal (i,i), we have a grid where each row i has missing cell at column i, each column i missing cell at row i. The remaining cells form two triangular regions: the region above the diagonal (i < j) and below (i > j). The region above diagonal includes cells (i,j) with i < j; region below includes (i,j) with i > j. These are like two triangles, each is a Young diagram shape. The region above diagonal is upper triangular (strictly). That region can be tiled by rectangles: we could tile each row i's cells to the right of diagonal (columns i+1..n). That would be n - i cells, contiguous horizontally. So each row i (i from 1..n) we could have a horizontal rectangle covering (i, i+1..n). That's n rows -> n rectangles (except maybe last row where none). Similarly for region below diagonal, we could tile each column j's cells below diagonal (rows j+1..n). That's also n rectangles. But note that this double counts the region? Actually the upper region is covered by horizontal rectangles per row; the lower region is covered by vertical rectangles per column. So total rectangles = (n-1) + (n-1) = 2n - 2? Wait, row i for i=1..n, there are n-i cells; for i=n, zero cells, so we can ignore row n. So we have n-1 horizontal rectangles. Similarly, for lower region: column j has cells with rows j+1..n, which is n-j cells; for j=n, zero, so n-1 vertical rectangles. Total rectangles = 2(n-1) = 2n - 2.\n\nBut can we do better? Possibly we can combine some of the horizontal and vertical rectangles into larger rectangles that cross the diagonal but avoid holes? However any rectangle that contains both cells above and below diagonal must cross the diagonal line (i=j) somewhere. To avoid holes, it must avoid the diagonal cells, which are holes. So any rectangle cannot contain any hole; thus it cannot include any cell (i,i). But it could be shaped like a rectangle that lies entirely in the region above diagonal (i<j), or entirely below diagonal (i>j), or maybe straddle the diagonal but with a missing column/row? Let's examine.\n\nIf we try to tile the whole region above diagonal with fewer than n-1 rectangles, how can we? The region above diagonal is a shape known as a Ferrers diagram: for each row i, there are n-i cells in that row. The shape is monotone decreasing in row index. The minimal number of axis-aligned rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram shape is known as the \"rectangle partition problem.\" The minimal number of rectangles to partition a monotone shape is equal to the number of \"corners\" or something. Actually, there's a known result: the minimum number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram (Ferrers shape) equals the size of the largest antichain in its poset of cells? Not sure. But there might be known results: the minimal number of rectangles to partition a monotone shape equals the number of \"steps\" in the shape. For the triangular shape, the minimal number of rectangles needed is n-1 perhaps. Let's test: For n=3 (3x3 grid with holes at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3)). The region above diagonal includes cells (1,2),(1,3),(2,3). That's shape: row1: 2 cells, row2:1 cell, row3:0. Minimal rectangles? Could we tile them with 2 rectangles? Yes: rectangle covering (1,2)-(1,3) (row 1, columns 2-3) and rectangle covering (2,3) (single cell). That's 2 rectangles = n-1 = 2. So matches.\n\nCould we tile with 1 rectangle? No, because shape is not rectangle; it's missing cell (2,2) which is a hole; but we can only tile region above diagonal, which is a shape that is not rectangular. So minimal rectangles = n-1.\n\nSimilarly the lower region also needs n-1 rectangles.\n\nThus total minimal = 2n - 2 = 2*2025 - 2 = 4050 - 2 = 4048? Wait 2*2025 = 4050, minus 2 = 4048. Actually 2n - 2 = 2*2025 - 2 = 4050 - 2 = 4048.\n\nBut is there any way to reduce total number of rectangles by using rectangles that cover cells from both above and below diagonal but avoid holes? Could we have a rectangle that covers a region that is like an L-shape? No, rectangle must be contiguous block of rows and columns. If it includes both i<j and i>j cells, then it must include some (i,i) cell (hole) because any rectangle with rows from a to b and columns from c to d includes the diagonal cell (i,i) if a \u2264 i \u2264 b and c \u2264 i \u2264 d simultaneously. So the rectangle intersects the diagonal at some row i that lies within both row interval and column interval. If the row interval and column interval intersect (i.e., there is an i such that a \u2264 i \u2264 b and c \u2264 i \u2264 d), then the rectangle includes (i,i). So to avoid holes, the rectangle's row interval and column interval must be disjoint: either all rows are less than all columns, or all columns are less than all rows. Let's define row interval [r1,r2] and column interval [c1,c2]. If r2 < c1, then all rows are less than all columns (i.e., rectangle lies strictly above diagonal). If c2 < r1, then rectangle lies strictly below diagonal. Otherwise they intersect, causing inclusion of a diagonal cell. So any rectangle must be either wholly above diagonal (all rows < all columns) or wholly below diagonal (all columns < all rows). So the above and below regions are separated by the diagonal; you cannot have rectangles crossing the diagonal.\n\nThus the tiling splits into two independent tilings: one covering the region above diagonal (strictly upper triangular), and one covering the region below diagonal (strictly lower triangular). So minimal number of rectangles = minimal for upper region + minimal for lower region. Both are symmetric.\n\nThus we need to find minimal number of rectangles to partition a strict upper triangular shape of size n x n, with cells (i,j) for i<j. The shape is known: it's an \"upper triangular shape\". Minimal rectangles needed is n-1: we can use one rectangle per row (or per column) as described. Could we use fewer? Let's explore combinatorial lower bound.\n\nWe can view each rectangle as covering a set of rows and columns. For the upper triangular shape, each rectangle has rows i..i' and columns j..j' such that i' < j (since all rows must be less than all columns). So rectangle's row interval and column interval are disjoint. The shape includes all pairs (i,j) with i<j. The region is essentially a complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} with missing diagonal edges? Actually treat rows and columns as two parts. The region includes all edges (i,j) where i (row) < j (column). This is like a tournament of size n but directed edges from lower index to higher index. Each rectangle corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph between sets of rows R and columns C where max(R) < min(C). So each rectangle covers all edges from a set of rows to a set of columns that are all larger.\n\nGoal: partition the edge set of a complete directed acyclic graph into minimal number of complete bipartite subgraphs with direction (i<j). This is a known problem: the minimum number of \"interval orders\" or something. Equivalent to covering the comparability graph of a total order with \"bipartite cliques\"? This is like partitioning the comparability matrix (strict total order) into monochromatic rectangles (i.e., \"staircase\" shape). There is known result: the Boolean matrix of a total order (i.e., the upper triangular matrix) has Boolean rank equal to n, or something? Let's examine.\n\nThe matrix A of size n x n with entries A_{i,j}=1 if i<j else 0. This is the strict order matrix (upper triangular ones). We ask: what is the Boolean rank of A? The Boolean rank of a binary matrix is the minimum number r such that A can be expressed as a Boolean product of an n x r and r x n binary matrices. This is equivalent to the minimum number of monochromatic rectangles needed to partition the ones (if rectangles are allowed to overlap? Actually, the covering number vs partition number?). There is known result that the Boolean rank of the n x n triangular matrix is \u2308log_2 n\u2309? Or something else? Let's verify. Wait, the Boolean rank of the matrix of all ones except diagonal zeros is known to be \u2308log_2 n\u2309? Might be for the \"disjointness\" matrix? Let's recall: The adjacency matrix of a complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} is all ones. Its Boolean rank is 1