al blocks above the diagonal (i.e., group rows less than column groups). Similarly rectangle covering rows groups [p+1..k] and columns groups [1..p] covers below diagonal. So we can tile the off-diagonal region with just 2 rectangles at group-level, independent of k. Indeed, choose p = some index; but we need to ensure each off-diagonal block is included exactly once. If we take rectangle covering rows groups 1..k and columns groups 1..k but excluding diagonal? That's not a rectangle because we must have contiguous intervals of rows and columns; the off-diagonal region is not a single rectangle because it's a union of two triangles (above and below diagonal). However, we can split into two rectangles: one covering all rows groups 1..k and columns groups 1..k but with rows less than columns? That's not a rectangle; we need a single rectangle covering all rows and all columns, which includes diagonal blocks (holes). So not allowed.\n\nBut we can partition off-diagonal region into two rectangles: one rectangle covering rows groups 1..k, columns groups (some contiguous segment) that does not intersect row groups. But row groups are entire set 1..k; any column interval overlapping any rows (which are all rows) will intersect at some indices where row group equals column group. Because rows include all indices. So to avoid diagonal, the column interval must be a subset of columns that does not intersect the rows set? But rows set includes all groups, so any column group will intersect at that index (since row groups contain that index). But the condition for rectangle is not that row groups and column groups be disjoint as sets of indices; it's that for each row group index i in row interval, the hole column group i must be outside column interval. Since each row group i's holes are only in its own column group i. So for any row group i in row interval, if column interval includes group i, then the rectangle includes some columns from group i, and for rows in group i, those columns intersect the hole block (i,i). Since holes are inside that block, the rectangle would contain holes. So we must avoid including column group i for any row group i in the row interval. Thus row interval and column interval must be such that they are disjoint as sets of indices. Since row interval includes many indices, column interval must avoid those indices. So column interval must be a contiguous interval of column groups that is entirely within the complement of row interval indices. If row interval is a contiguous segment [a,b], then column interval must be a contiguous segment that lies entirely in [1,a-1] or [b+1,k]. So we can have rectangle covering rows groups [a,b] and columns groups [b+1,k] or [1,a-1]. That yields a rectangle covering top-right or bottom-left off-diagonal region relative to the selected row interval.\n\nThus we can partition the off-diagonal region into at most 2(k-1) rectangles: For each possible row interval we could cover a block. But we can do more clever partitioning.\n\nActually, we can tile the off-diagonal region using (2k - 2) rectangles at group level, analogous to the original triangular tiling but now each rectangle is a super-rectangle covering multiple groups. For each group index i, we can have rectangle covering rows groups i..k and columns groups i+1..k (above diagonal) -> that's k-1 rectangles. Similarly, rectangles covering rows groups i+1..k and columns groups i..i (?), not exactly. Let's think.\n\nBut we could also tile each off-diagonal block individually (k(k-1) blocks) using each block as a rectangle (size k x k). That's k(k-1) rectangles, which is O(k^2) = n, larger than 2n-2 = O(n). So not beneficial.\n\nThus the grouping approach does not reduce complexity at group level; we still have O(k^2) rectangles needed to cover off-diagonal region, unless we can combine groups into larger rectangles crossing many groups at once. But the constraint that row interval and column interval must be disjoint sets of 